se,"_score_margin_advantage":40.4,"_gate_pool_share":null,"_gp_pool_share":null,"_score_pool_share":null,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":0.2650098217471979,"_gp_fcf_durability":true,"_score_fcf_durability":100.0,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":0,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.1800499369220253,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":100.0,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.08759318783290815,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":100.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.012680800475530017,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":78.9,"_gate_insider_own":0.021289999,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":14.2,"_gate_insider_buying":0.222,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":22.2,"pf":[],"description_full":"Hilton Grand Vacations Inc. develops, markets, sells, manages, and operates the resorts, timeshare plans, and ancillary reservation services under the Hilton Grand Vacations brand in the United States, Japan, and Europe. The company operates through two segments: Real Estate Sales and Financing, and Resort Operations and Club Management segments. The Real Estate Sales and Financing segment market and sells the VOIs, and source VOIs through fee-for-service agreements; and provides consumer financing and services loans. The Resort Operations and Club Management segment manages and operates the clubs which provides exchange, leisure travel, and reservation services, as well as engages in the rental of inventory made available due to ownership exchanges through its club programs, and provides ancillary services including food and beverage, retail and spa at timeshare properties. Hilton Grand Vacations Inc. was founded in 1992 and is headquartered in Orlando, Florida.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mark D. Wang | CEO & Director | Age 68 | Compensation: $6.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 22K people. Revenue per employee of $226K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers, improving at 12%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $10K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 28\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Hilton Grand Vacations Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $3.3B market cap on $5.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +10% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $230M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 3.70\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 5.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.6\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 18.0% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 2.1% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.8) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (16.3% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins softening -1.7pp YoY \u2014 early warning on the moat","sev":"amber","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (78% sell ratio, net $14.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Consumer Cyclical peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 61% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Valued at a 22% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Consumer Cyclical sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Zacks Research Upgrades Hilton Grand Vacations (NYSE:HGV) to Hold","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/05/zacks-research-upgrades-hilton-grand-vacations-nysehgv-to-hold.html","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":1793884620.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.856,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["analyst_action"]},{"title":"Insider Buying: Hilton Grand Vacations (NYSE:HGV) Insider Purchases 12,200 Shares of Stock","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/04/insider-buying-hilton-grand-vacations-nysehgv-insid